# IT admin guardrails

Write, change and review PowerShell for IT administration (Microsoft Graph, Microsoft 365, Entra ID, Intune, Active Directory, Exchange Online) so it is read-only or dry-run by default. A bundled checker enforces the mechanical rules.

## Ground rules

1. Read before you write. Start read-only. Add a change only when asked.
2. Every change is a dry run until someone says otherwise: SupportsShouldProcess, ShouldProcess before each change, default to a dry run, explicit -Apply.
3. Act on a named, reviewed list, never "all users". Cap changes with -MaxChanges and show the count first.
4. No secrets in a script, a log or a chat. Interactive sign-in, a certificate or a vault.
5. Least permission: read scripts ask for Read scopes. Name every scope and why.
6. Always needs a person's yes: wipe or delete devices, role and group privilege, mail forwarding and delegation, authentication methods, Conditional Access, switching a protection off.
7. Leave a trail: a CSV or log of every object and result. Stop on the first unexpected error.
8. Never run the script for the person unless asked. Show the script, the checker result and what a dry run does.
9. Never invent tenant names, domains, ids or accounts. Use parameters.

## Steps

1. Ask, one question at a time: what changes, for which named accounts or devices, who approves.
2. Write the read-only version first. Its output is the list the person reviews.
3. Write the change version with SupportsShouldProcess, a $Apply switch, $WhatIfPreference set when not applying, -MaxChanges, try/catch and a log.
4. Run `node {{SCRIPTS}}/check-ps.mjs <file-or-folder>`. Fix every error; fix or explain every warning.
5. Override a rule only with `# guardrail-allow PSG0xx: reason` naming who approved it. Never to make the checker pass.
6. Report: what it does, what a dry run shows, scopes needed, checker result, what you could not verify, what needs approval before -Apply.

A clean checker result means no known pattern was found, not that the script is safe.
